\section{The Z scale}{
Results are reported on the \eqn{L = Z^{-1}L^*} scale, where
\eqn{Z = \mathrm{E}w^2/\mathrm{E}w} on a test mortality. \eqn{Z} is a change
of units, chosen so that one parameter costs about one unit of \eqn{L}, and
it must be the same for every model being compared -- otherwise the
penalised log-likelihoods are not on one scale and the comparison means
nothing.

It is set in one of two ways, never from the model's own reference mortality,
and they are alternatives rather than a pair:
\itemize{
\item \code{test_mortality} names the mortality \eqn{Z} is measured on, over this same
data, weight and include. It defaults to \code{\link[=default_mortality]{default_mortality()}}, and a fixed
shared choice is worth more here than a good one, since \eqn{Z}'s job is to
be one yardstick.
\item \code{Z} gives \eqn{Z} directly as a number, skipping the measurement.
This is what model selection needs, the caller having obtained it once and
passed it to every candidate, and \code{\link[=compare_models]{compare_models()}} does exactly that.
}

Where the weight is absent, is the literal 1, or takes only the values 0 and
1 -- a logical expression, or a column holding nothing but zeros and ones --
\eqn{w^2 = w} makes \eqn{Z} exactly one. The engine recognises such a weight
and accumulates \eqn{\mathrm{E}w^2} as \eqn{\mathrm{E}w} itself, so the
measured \eqn{Z} is one to the last bit rather than to a tolerance. The
measurement is made regardless, since the same pass over the data supplies
the population figures that \code{print()} reports.
}

\section{Convergence}{
The iteration stops when the gain in \eqn{L} still available falls below
\code{L_tolerance}, which is a forward-looking test: it measures the distance left to
the optimum rather than the size of the last step, so it is not fooled by a
damped step or by a model whose maximum does not exist. Stopping within
\eqn{\epsilon} of the maximum leaves the coefficients within
\eqn{\sqrt{2\epsilon/I}} of theirs.
}

